The L6 Architectural Strategy: Tombstoning & The "Logical vs. Physical" Split
A Staff-level engineer knows that re-architecting a global backup system to be real-time is a "Sunk Cost" trap. Instead, we must decouple Visibility from Storage.
1. The Multi-Tiered Deletion Flow (The "Tombstone" Pattern)
We cannot re-run 7-day batch jobs every hour. Instead, we introduce a Global Deletion Registry (GDR).
•	Tier 1: Immediate Metadata Invalidation (The "Logical" Delete): Within milliseconds of a user clicking delete, the file is marked with a "Tombstone" in the live metadata layer. The user and APIs see the file as gone.
•	Tier 2: The Filtered Backup Access Layer: We modify the Backup Recovery Service. If a restore is triggered, the system must cross-reference every file against the GDR. If a file is in the GDR, it is skipped during the "Restore" process. This guarantees compliance (the data is never "restored") while buying time for the physical deletion.
2. The "Physical" Deletion Optimization
To handle the 24-hour physical requirement without 7-day batch overhead, we move to a Compacted Incremental Clean-up:
•	Sharded Deletion Logs: We shard the GDR by the same partition key as our backup blocks.
•	In-Place Punching: Instead of rewriting a 10TB backup blob to delete a 1MB file, we use "File Hole Punching" (supported by many distributed file systems) to deallocate the specific blocks occupied by the deleted file within the backup volume. This meets the "physically deleted" requirement with $O(1)$ metadata operations rather than $O(N)$ data rewrites.
3. The L6 Business Trade-off: The "Compliance Tax"
I would present the following to stakeholders:
•	The Cost: We will see a 2-5% increase in storage "slack" (fragmentation) due to hole-punching rather than full compaction.
•	The Risk: The GDR becomes a Tier-0 Dependency. If the GDR is down, restores must be paused to prevent accidental data resurrection.
•	The Recommendation: We accept the fragmentation cost to avoid the massive egress/ingress costs of daily full-backup rewrites.
