The "Structured Data" Bridge: Dynamic Tool-Use & Semantic Mapping
Instead of Text-to-SQL (which is prone to joining the wrong tables and causing massive "Full Table Scans" on production databases), I would architect a Semantic API Gateway.
1. The Architectural Strategy: Function Calling over Text-to-SQL
We define a set of Validated Analytical Functions (e.g., get_department_payroll_variance, fetch_individual_withholding_history).
•	The Workflow: The LLM acts as the Router. It identifies the intent (e.g., "Why did my payroll increase this month?") and maps it to a specific, pre-optimized API tool.
•	Safety: The LLM never writes raw SQL. It only provides the parameters for the API (e.g., user_id, period_start). This prevents SQL injection and ensures the query plan is pre-indexed and performance-tuned.
2. The "Schema-to-Semantic" Mapping (The Bridge)
To help the LLM understand which tool to use, we maintain a Semantic Metadata Layer.
•	We use LLM-optimized documentation for our API endpoints. Instead of just "Endpoint: /v1/payroll," we describe it: "Use this to retrieve gross pay, tax deductions, and net pay for a specific employee ID. Do NOT use for aggregate department reports."
•	This metadata allows the Agent to choose the most efficient "path" to the truth.
3. Solving for "Stale Snapshots": The "Freshness" Check
For mission-critical payroll queries, I would implement a Two-Stage Retrieval:
•	Stage 1 (Context): Retrieve the "Policy" from the RAG Engine (e.g., "How is the 401k match calculated?").
•	Stage 2 (Real-Time Fact): Trigger the API Gateway to pull the Live Ledger for the specific user.
•	Synthesizer: The Nexus-Intelligence layer then combines the "Policy" and the "Live Fact" to generate the answer: "According to the 2026 Policy [Link], your 401k match is 5%. Based on your live payroll record from today, your contribution was $X, resulting in a $Y match."
